WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.658 --> 00:00:04.480
- [Narrator] With a lifespan
able to reach as old as 69,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.400
these iguanas can live as long as humans.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.770
This is the Grand Cayman blue iguana.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.270
(light music)

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.990
Found only in Grand Cayman,
the largest and western most

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.060
of the Cayman Islands,
their blue skin becomes more

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.170
prominent when establishing territory

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.710
when other iguanas are nearby.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.420
Males turn especially bright
blue during breeding season,

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.570
but their blue skin isn't
their only defining feature.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.480
They have striking red
eyes and excellent vision,

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.900
which enables them to
notice shapes and movement

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.283
at long distances.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.080
Listed as endangered
due to human activity,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.670
habitat loss, and being preyed upon by

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.070
free-roaming predators such as dogs,

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.560
cats, and rats, these
beautiful blue lizards

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.290
face an uphill battle against extinction.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.853
This is the Grand Cayman blue iguana.

00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:06.295
(light music)

